- [ ] No memory leaks

### Sign-off
- [ ] All tests passed
- [ ] Ready for deployment
```

## When to Skip Functional Testing

**NEVER.** Always test.

Even for:
- ❌ "Just a CSS change" → Test the feature
- ❌ "Just a typo fix" → Test the feature  
- ❌ "Just refactoring" → Test the feature
- ❌ "Just adding comments" → Test that code still works

## Automated Testing (Future)

### Priority 1: E2E Tests for Critical Paths
```typescript
// e2e/marketplace-comparison.spec.ts
test('marketplace comparison works', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/marketplace')
  
  // Select items
  await page.click('[data-testid="compare-btn-1"]')
  await page.click('[data-testid="compare-btn-2"]')
  
  // Open comparison
  await page.click('[data-testid="compare-button"]')
  
  // Verify modal shows content
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="comparison-content"]')).toBeVisible()
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="comparison-table"]')).toBeVisible()
})
```

### Priority 2: Component Tests
```typescript
// components/marketplace/comparison-modal.test.tsx  
describe('ComparisonModal', () => {
  it('shows error when items have no data', () => {
    const { getByText } = render(
      <ComparisonModal 
        open={true} 
        items={[{ id: '1', title: '' }]} 
      />
    )
    expect(getByText(/cannot compare/i)).toBeInTheDocument()
  })
})
```

## Integration with Deployment Workflow

### Updated Deployment Checklist
1. ✅ Code changes complete
2. ✅ Code-level checks pass (TS, lint, build)
3. ✅ **Functional tests pass** ← NEW REQUIREMENT
4. ✅ Regression tests pass
5. ✅ Commit and push
6. ✅ Monitor deployment
7. ✅ Verify in production

### Git Pre-Push Hook (Optional)
```bash
#!/bin/bash
# .git/hooks/pre-push

echo "🧪 Running pre-push tests..."

# Run build
npm run build || exit 1

echo "✅ Build passed"
echo "⚠️  Remember to test features manually before deploying!"
echo "See .cursor/rules/testing-before-deployment.mdc"
```

## Accountability

### Before Deployment
- [ ] I have tested ALL affected features manually
- [ ] I have tested error cases and edge cases
- [ ] I have checked browser console for errors
- [ ] I have tested on mobile viewport (if UI change)
- [ ] I am confident this will not break production

### If Something Breaks
1. **Immediate**: Revert the deployment
2. **Root cause**: Why wasn't it caught in testing?
3. **Prevention**: Update this doc with new test case
4. **Re-test**: Before re-deploying

## Examples of Good Testing

### Example 1: CSS Change to Button
```
Change: Updated button hover color
Testing:
✅ Clicked button - still works
✅ Hovered button - color correct
✅ Button disabled state - works
✅ Button in different contexts - works
✅ Light/dark mode - both work
```

### Example 2: Server Action Update  
```
Change: Added authorization check to updateTask()
Testing:
✅ Update task as owner - works
✅ Update task as team member - works  
✅ Update task from different foundry - blocked ✓
✅ Update task without auth - blocked ✓
✅ Console shows no errors
```

### Example 3: Database Migration
```
Change: Added index to report_snapshots table
Testing:
✅ Migration applied successfully
✅ Queries using indexed column are faster
✅ No orphaned records  
✅ FK constraints work
✅ RLS policies still enforced
```

## Summary

**The Golden Rule**: If you touch it, you test it. Every time. No exceptions.

**Cost of skipping**: Broken production feature, user frustration, time spent debugging and fixing

**Cost of testing**: 5-10 minutes per feature

The math is simple: Always test.
